XFER(1)                      User Commands

NAME
    xfer - transfer files between the guest and the host

SYNOPSIS
    xfer put [-h HOST] [-p PORT] PATH...
    xfer get [-h HOST] [-p PORT] [-o DIR] NAME...
    xfer ls [-h HOST] [-p PORT] [DIR]
    xfer serve [-p PORT] [DIR]

DESCRIPTION
    Copies files and directories over TCP. HOST defaults to 10.0.2.2,
    the host machine as seen from QEMU user networking, and PORT to 9101,
    the port of "tools/xfer.py serve" on the host. So "xfer put notes.txt"
    in the guest sends a file to the host, "xfer get build.tar" fetches
    one, and "xfer ls" lists the served directory. A directory is
    transferred file by file under its name, and empty directories are
    created as well. Every transfer carries a CRC32 checked by both ends.

    serve makes DIR (default the current directory) available on PORT,
    by default 9100. The host reaches it through the port forward that
    "make run" sets up from host port 9100, with "tools/xfer.py put
    FILE". The server writes an upload to a temporary file and replaces
    the target only after a complete and correct transfer, so a failed
    upload leaves an existing file intact. Paths in requests are
    relative and may not contain "..". A connection without progress for
    30 seconds is dropped. While a transfer runs, every side whose standard
    error is a terminal (the client, the server's child and tools/xfer.py)
    prints the bytes done and the throughput once per second; a summary
    line follows when the transfer completes.

OPTIONS
    -h HOST    server address
    -p PORT    server port (default 9101, and 9100 for serve)
    -o DIR     directory that get writes into (default .)

EXAMPLES
    xfer put /home/report.txt
    xfer get -o /tmp photos
    xfer serve /home

SEE ALSO
    nc(1), http(1), net(1), index(7)
